  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 931 PM MST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nUNSEASONABLY WARM AND DRY THROUGH FRIDAY:\n\nHigh pressure will persist through the work week bringing \nunseasonably warm and dry conditions. Temperatures are expected to \nrun 5-10 degrees above normal through Friday.\n\nCOOLER, WETTER THIS WEEKEND:\n\nBy Friday afternoon, high pressure will have moved east of the \nDivide, and flow aloft will shift southwesterly. This will begin to \nadvect in rich subtropical moisture. A quick moving wave will track \ninland through the Great Basin Friday night into Saturday, acting on \nthis rich moisture. Valley rain, mountain snow above 8k-9k \nfeet, and an isolated thunderstorm or two are expected through\nthe day Saturday. The highest probability of accumulating snow \nremains over the San Juans, generally above 10k feet. The \nlikelihood of 6+ inches of snow for the San Juans remains high \n(70-80%), while the likelihood of 12 or more inches remains low \n(<20%). While the Central Colorado mountains are the next most \nlikely areas to see accumulating snow of 6+ inches, the NBM \nprobabilities keep that likelihood on the lower end (<30%). As \nhigh res guidance comes in over the next 24 hours, we'll get a \nbetter handle of snow totals and timing or greatest impacts. \nSlippery to hazardous travel will be possible over the mountains\npasses Saturday into Sunday. Snow levels are expected to drop \nto 6k-7k feet by Sunday morning, however the bulk of the \nprecipitation will be over by then, with only lingering \norographic showers expected. Temperatures through the weekend \nwill run near-normal for mid- November.\n\nUNSETTLED INTO NEXT WEEK:\n\nThe wavy, unsettled pattern continues into next week. Models are \nindicating the potential for another round of mountain snow and \nvalley rain in the Tuesday to Wednesday timeframe. Confidence on the \ndetails of this potential system remains low, however, as a wide \nvariety of solutions exist regarding the track, timing, and strength \nof this system.",
